model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Accounts and pool displays follow committed scoped revisions through the existing app-data supervisor transport. Hosted account, observation, link, group, role, sharing and session writers queue dirty keys after their domain writes. One transaction marker schedules one initially deferred constraint trigger; at commit it applies heads and pending delivery in sorted scope, topic and resource order. Domain rows always precede journal locks, including duplicate provisioning, first-time defaults and legacy binding backfill. Deployment and external producer revisions use the same queue. Repeated keys coalesce per transaction and rollback leaves no heads, pending delivery or markers. App-source connection enabled state, selection payloads and revocations invalidate account and app projections, including encrypted payload writes without a row revision bump. Reporting, driver-check and execution-target state invalidate account projections; reporting leases alone do not. Unsupported app probes expose no first claim and publish no revision. Known collectors retain their genuine collecting state; legacy declarations publish only settled first results. Optional observation metadata remains compatible with released declaration decoders. Empty links, initial accounts and unrelated organization metadata do not invalidate usage; retained reports, authority, credentials and source changes still do. Home readings and request history map to the actual pool app ID; derived usage, capacity, limit and price changes record their revisions and pending delivery with the runtime adapter source commit. Repository initialization changes editable source readiness without changing the active version, selected accounts or authority, so repository-only writes create no account or app revision. Identical writes and collection bookkeeping do not create revisions, and retry discovery adds no statement to unchanged pool upkeep. The production PoolDatabase release command checks every existing Home trigger target owner as postgres before any ownership alteration, transfers only postgres-owned targets to the stable owner, and rejects any other owner with its typed table and owner error before model-history migration. Already-owned targets and repeat runs alter nothing; inherited runtime membership preserves reads and writes. Capture retains old-schema collection before the additive pending table exists, checks readiness within its existing timeout-setting query, and includes pending changes as soon as the table is available. Registration precedes the authoritative handshake. One accepted read pairs each body with its principal-bound revision receipt. The server authorizes and fences the first document read; hydration reuses that accepted pair without a second body request. Unmounted views retain only the accepted pair, with no raw body, live subscription or polling. A new connection confirms retained authority and heads before showing that pair; only the first hydration can reuse the request-bound document immediately. Question and run updates stay reactive. Intent settles from those authorized queue reads alone; the mounted view resolves app labels through the protected directory without holding the whole queue behind its fence. Pending names remain pending, and a directory authority denial still clears protected content across warm remounts while renewed authority is pending. Prefetch retains no directory watch. A browser accepts a body only between matching current revision reads under one opaque principal/session identity, rejects older in-flight work, and clears protected data on denial. Overview usage, personal usage and capacity publish independently only after their own revision fences; a combined capacity verdict requires matching accepted head versions within one refresh. A slow capacity response never holds usage or its retry. A fresh pool view starts its source read after the caller revision baseline and reloads process-local group metadata that another Home writer may have changed. Authority changes replace paged history and invalidate old pagination; observation freshness expires locally from original source deadlines. Hidden views abort pending readers and propagate hidden cancellation through the revision fence without a trailing head read; reconnect compares durable heads and semantic expiry deadlines. An absolute session deadline rechecks authority once and resyncs renewed sessions; heartbeat frames perform no database reads. Current accepted data remains visible only while an actual refresh is in flight or with its original observation and failure state. Unconverted app-version and generic-query reconciliation timers remain in place. A transaction notifies the durable delivery pump only when committed keys exist. A scoped PostgreSQL listener drains pending rows at startup and after each non-empty notification batch, with bounded failure retry. One notification alone starts delivery; buffered notifications coalesce without waiting for another commit. Ordinary tool calls add no empty delivery query. A delivery pass has one interruptible ten-second budget, below the fifteen-second process stop allowance. The native HTTP serving scope closes revision streams before listener draining, so an open stream cannot outlive the process stop allowance. Interrupted SDK and recovery work starts no delivery. Unacknowledged rows survive shutdown and the next process can reacquire the data lock and deliver them. Retained fixtures remove dependent change triggers, schema and migration journal entries together before reconstructing an earlier layout. A TLS fixture propagates a failed open response by closing it and sends 502 only before headers are sent. The final combined MCP renderer is generated after all shared revision-client and presentation carries. Model-history migrations check each exact name, so the missing v5 change trigger migration applies after retained v6, v7 and v8 without replaying them or rewriting history. Installed catalog instances do not expire with age; outdated-snapshot fixtures publish a committed app revision before the protected inventory rereads them. Provisioning status changes publish the directory revision, and synthetic stop and retry responses supply that signal. Photo-failure proofs await the failed image request rather than page-wide idle while a revision stream is open. Change feeds coexist with imported account-label writes, preserving each protected route and action. Both hosted and self-hosted transports obey the same contract."
    }
  }
}
